                }
            },
            "text": "Writes an array to a file in numpy's own format, exactly as it is: every value, the shape, the number type. np.load reads it back.\n\nA .npy file holds one array. A path ending in .npz writes the numpy archive format instead, with the array under the name data - the format np.load and most motion- capture archives use. Without an extension, .npy is added.\n\nIt is the way to keep something a patch has computed - a recorded take, a calibration, a fitted model, a set of poses - and use it again later or elsewhere.\n\nWRITING ONLY WHEN ASKED:\nAn array arriving at in is only held, not written: a stream of arrays does not rewrite the file sixty times a second. The file is written when write is clicked (or sent a bang).\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.save <path>\nDefault: no path - the first write asks where to save.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.save recordings/take_1.npy\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array to save (a list is turned into one). Held until written; each new one replaces the last.\n\nwrite (button, also an inlet):\nWrites the held array to the path. With no path yet, it asks where, as save as does.\n\nsave as (button, also an inlet):\nAsks for a file name and folder, writes there, and keeps that path for the next write.\n\npath:\nAn option, under the node: where write saves. A path that is not absolute is relative to the folder the app was started from. It is saved with the patch.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsaved path:\nThe full path of the file, sent each time it is written - ready to pass on, or to show where the file went.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour values arrive and are held. Click write (on the node) and choose where to save; the path appears below. Nothing is saved until you do, and nothing is saved in the help folder.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.load reads the file back. np.savetxt writes the other format."
        },
